a stub must still answer in the right register

NSUserActivity is a forwarding stub whose methodSignatureForSelector answered v@: for every
selector. A void return writes nothing, so an initialiser hands back whatever the previous
call left in the return register, and the caller has an object that is not one.

iA Writer took that value, 0x4e, and sent it setEligibleForHandoff:. The result is a SIGSEGV
inside objc_msgSend with no frame chain, no exception, and nothing in the log naming
NSUserActivity at all. The receiver and the selector were still in the registers the signal
context carries, which is how it was found.

@@: with an explicit nil costs a caller that wanted void nothing, and gives one that wanted
an object the only safe answer: messaging nil is defined and messaging 0x4e is not.

The same template is used by 9806 generated stub files in this tree, so this is one instance
of a defect that is present everywhere and fires only when a caller uses the return value.

--- a/src/NSUserActivity.m
+++ b/src/NSUserActivity.m
@@ -25,13 +25,26 @@
 
 @implementation NSUserActivity
 
+/*
+ * A STUB STILL HAS TO ANSWER IN THE RIGHT REGISTER.
+ *
+ * "v@:" says every method returns nothing, so an initialiser writes nothing and the caller keeps
+ * whatever the previous call left in the return register. iA Writer took that value, 0x4e, for its
+ * new activity and sent it setEligibleForHandoff:, which is a SIGSEGV inside objc_msgSend with no
+ * frame chain and nothing in the log to connect it to NSUserActivity.
+ *
+ * "@@:" with an explicit nil costs a caller that wanted void nothing and gives one that wanted an
+ * object the only safe answer, because messaging nil is defined and messaging 0x4e is not.
+ */
 - (NSMethodSignature *)methodSignatureForSelector:(SEL)aSelector
 {
-    return [NSMethodSignature signatureWithObjCTypes: "v@:"];
+    return [NSMethodSignature signatureWithObjCTypes: "@@:"];
 }
 
 - (void)forwardInvocation:(NSInvocation *)anInvocation
 {
+    id nothing = nil;
+    [anInvocation setReturnValue: &nothing];
     NSLog(@"Stub called: %@ in %@", NSStringFromSelector([anInvocation selector]), [self class]);
 }
 
